    <CollateralDetail>
      <TextContent>
        <TextType>03</TextType>
        <ContentAudience>00</ContentAudience>
        <Text>Why can't we predict the weather past two weeks? Chaos theory is the science of deterministic unpredictability: simple nonlinear rules whose sensitivity to starting conditions makes long-range forecasting impossible in principle — while leaving stable statistics, strange fractal attractors, and honest horizons in its place. From Poincaré's three-body problem and Lorenz's butterfly effect to Feigenbaum's universal constants, the control of chaos, and ensemble forecasting, this is the complete story of the mathematics that taught the modern world the limits of prediction.</Text>
      </TextContent>
